# Per-machine toolchain paths (TASS / PYTHON overrides)
source/local.make

# Per-machine Claude Code state: settings.local.json holds this box's tool
# permissions, session-todo.json is scratch. Neither belongs to the project.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/session-todo.json

# Emulator memory dumps (x16emu writes dump*.bin, Box16 writes dump*.txt)
dump*.bin
dump*.txt
memory_stats.txt

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Transient: pcode.py emits scripts/pcodeconstraw.py here, the Makefile then
# moves it to common-scripts/. The directory itself must exist to build.
source/common-source/scripts/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output.
#
# build/ holds a MIX of checked-in sources (hello.bas, balls.bas, PROTEUS7.PRG)
# and generated files, so it cannot be ignored wholesale -- only the products.
#
# code.lbl and code.lst are the worst offenders: 64tass emits their symbols in a
# nondeterministic order, so they churned on every single build even when nothing
# changed. (code.prg is byte-identical across rebuilds -- the codegen itself is
# deterministic; it is only these dumps that are not.)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
source/*/build/code.lbl
source/*/build/code.lst
source/*/build/code.prg
source/*/build/bank.prg
source/*/build/dump.bin
source/*/build/*.pcode
source/*/build/*.tokenised

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated by the randomised compiler-runtime suites.
#
# Each run writes a fresh random program (REM *** SEED nnnn ***), so all of this
# changed on every test run. examples/*.bas is just a copy of the LAST run's
# program -- a record, not an input.
#
# NOT ignored here: source/runtime/testing/code/code.bin. It looks like a test
# intermediate (the suite overwrites it with CODE.BIN and replays it via
# "make -C source/runtime run"), but runtime/testing/testend.asm does
# .binary "code/code.bin" -- it is a REQUIRED BUILD INPUT, and untracking it
# breaks "make libs" on a fresh clone. A plain build never rewrites it; only the
# randomised suite does, and that is a deliberate act whose diff is worth seeing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
source/unit-tests/compiler-runtime/build/
source/unit-tests/compiler-runtime/examples/*.bas
source/unit-tests/compiler-runtime/CODE.BIN

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# source/gpc front end. GPC.BASL (BASLOAD source) is tokenised into drive/GPC.SRC.PRG by
# build_basl.py driving the emulator, and GPC.BIN then COMPILES that into drive/GPC.PRG --
# GPC.BASL is written in GP.BASIC, so the tokenised form is compile-only and only the compiled
# GPC.PRG can be run. GPC.BASL and GPC.PRG SHIP and are tracked in drive/; the intermediate
# and its #SYMFILE do not. Ignore those, plus the tokenise scratch (a one-line driver, an
# echo log). The retired Prog8 front end sits in source/gpc/old-archive/: its .P8
# source and bump script are kept (tracked), but not its binary or build scratch.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
drive/GPCBLD.BAS
drive/GPCBLD.LOG
drive/BASLDONE
drive/GPC.SRC.PRG
drive/GPC.SRC.SYM

# GPC-BASIC/GPB.INC.BL is copied into drive/ by source/gpc/Makefile, because BASLOAD
# resolves GPC.BASL's #INCLUDE off the drive and cannot see GPC-BASIC/. GPC-BASIC/ is
# the master; this copy is a build artifact and must not be edited or tracked, or the two
# will drift and the tokenise will quietly use the stale one.
drive/GPB.INC.BL
source/gpc/old-archive/GPC.PRG
source/gpc/old-archive/build/

# The release used to be bundled into drive/blitz.zip (mkzip.py). That was dropped:
# nothing consumed it, and it was a git-tracked binary rebuilt on every release.
drive/blitz.zip

# The packaged download is built by release.sh / release.bat into the release/ drop folder
# as gpc-release-<n>.zip (see the gpc-release-*.zip rule below) -- a rebuilt-on-demand
# artifact, not something to commit. "release.zip" is the pre-2026-07-18 root name, kept
# ignored so any old local build lying around doesn't get committed.
release.zip

# GPC.INPUT is generated next to the compiler by "make -C source/application";
# the one that ships is drive/GPC.INPUT.
source/application/GPC.INPUT

# The engine's build stamp. buildnum.txt IS tracked -- it is the counter, and the single
# source of truth that release.sh reads. version.asm is generated from it by
# scripts/bumpbuild.py on every engine build, so committing it would only duplicate the
# number and churn the diff. bumpbuild.py creates the directory as well as the file (git
# cannot track an empty one), and it runs before build.py, so a fresh clone still builds.
source/application/source/generated/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# drive/ is now the shipping release directory (moved here from release/) AND
# the scratch area for batch-testing user BASIC programs. The release artifacts
# in drive/ ARE tracked; only the two scratch subdirs below are ignored:
# drive/prg-batch/ is the queue (drop source PRGs in), drive/archive/ holds
# the ones already run. Both are transient personal test material -- inputs we
# compile to find compiler gaps, not part of the project or the build.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
drive/prg-batch/
drive/archive/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ...AND the build's own output, which that opt-out policy kept sweeping in. A
# file left lying in drive/ becomes tracked on the next "git add -A", so the
# compiler's scratch had quietly accumulated: OBJECT.PRG and SOURCE.PRG (the
# names source/application/Makefile writes and deletes on every run), M.GPC.ERR
# (regenerated by every "make -C source/gpc release"), and 68K of flight-sim
# binaries that rode in with an unrelated commit -- all of them reproducible
# from sources already in the tree, and samples/FSIM16_V1/ held the originals.
#
# GPC.IMG.nnn.BIN joins the GPB/GPC.RT.nnn.BIN runtimes in being ignored rather
# than tracked. It is build-numbered, so every engine bump orphaned the previous
# one; make libs rebuilds it, and release.sh already says "build first" if it is
# not there. GP1.IMG.nnn.BIN, the bank code installed beside it, goes the same way.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
drive/OBJECT.PRG
drive/SOURCE.PRG
drive/C.*.PRG
drive/M.*
drive/*.LOG
drive/GPC.IMG.*.BIN
drive/GP1.IMG.*.BIN
samples/*/GPC.IMG.*.BIN
samples/*/GP1.IMG.*.BIN

# The resident runtimes, GPC.RT.<build>.BIN (with the GPB handlers) and
# GPC.RC.<build>.BIN (without them -- both are slices of one build, see common.inc).
# NOT tracked, and they cannot usefully be: the file name carries the RUNTIME BUILD
# NUMBER (scripts/rtname.py), so tracking them means new binaries in the tree
# and a pile of stale ones nobody asks for -- ten had accumulated by 17th Aug 2026,
# two of them tracked since 2nd Aug and eleven ABI bumps out of date. A stale one is
# worse than none: it carries a magic the bootstrap may accept and an opcode map that
# no longer matches. Build the current one with
#     make -C source/runtime gpc-rt
# which writes it into drive/ named for whatever engine build is checked out.
#   Two files now, and the names swapped at RT_ABI 20: GPB.RT is the FULL image (handlers
#   AND core, loading at RTGPBASE) and GPC.RT is the core alone. GPC.RC is the name the full
#   one used to have; kept here so an old one lying about in a working tree stays ignored.
#   GP1.RT is the third, the bank code both load into bank 1 (HANDLER-BANK).
drive/GPB.RT.*.BIN
drive/GPC.RT.*.BIN
drive/GP1.RT.*.BIN
drive/GPC.RC.*.BIN

# The GP.BASIC library is MASTERED in GPC-BASIC/ (see its README). BASLOAD resolves
# #INCLUDE by bare filename off the emulator's drive, and drive/ IS that drive, so
# building an example means copying the master here first. Those copies -- and the
# PRGs BASLOAD writes from them -- are staging and build output, not sources. Tracking
# them would put two copies of every library file in the repo, free to drift apart.
#   The GPC-BASIC masters are staged into drive/ to be built and are NOT tracked
#   there. Globbed rather than listed one by one: naming them individually is how
#   THEME.INC.BL and APPSYS.INC.BL got committed by accident, which is the same
#   class of mistake 15208f3 had to back out.
drive/*.INC.BL
drive/*.EXP.BL

#   The same staging one level up -- a PROGRAM's source is copied in beside the modules it
#   #INCLUDEs, because BASLOAD resolves both off the drive -- and the objects built from
#   the pair. Both used to be enumerated, a line per program, and the same lesson landed
#   again: GPBMODS.PRG and GPB.HELP.PRG were untracked AND unignored, which is how they
#   went unnoticed while drive/ grew to 13 MB. Globbed now, with the handful that are
#   NOT build output named back in. drive/readme.md says what is kept and why.
drive/*.PRG
drive/*.BASL
!drive/BASLOAD-GPC.PRG
!drive/DIR.PRG
!drive/GPC.PRG
!drive/POINTER.PRG
!drive/GPC.BASL

#   HELP-TXT/ is the viewer's data, xcopied from samples/GPC-HELP/HELP-TXT (readme.md line
#   106) so GPB.HELP.PRG can open //HELP-TXT/:NAME off the drive. Sixty files, byte-identical
#   to the master, and regenerated by samples/GPC-HELP/GPB.HELP.BASL -- a mirror, like
#   drive/samples/ below, not a source.
drive/HELP-TXT/

# demo/ is build output for menu-demo.bat and bmx-demo.bat: compiled objects
# plus BMX images copied out of samples/. Every byte of it is regenerable and
# the images are already tracked in samples/BMXVIEWER/SAMPLES, so tracking it
# would put half a megabyte of duplicates in the repo. The two .bat files at
# the root ARE tracked, and say how to rebuild what they run.
demo/

# drive/MD5 is the Commodore BASIC MD5 program, tracked: it is the fixture for the
# md5 regression test (source/unit-tests/md5), which compiles it, runs it BOTH compiled
# and interpreted, and fails if the two digests differ. The object, the map and the
# fixture bytes are all regenerated by that test on every run, so they are not.
drive/C.MD5
drive/M.MD5
drive/MD5FIX.DAT
drive/MD5DRV.TXT
drive/MD5*.LOG

# drive/samples/ is a build artifact: the root-level samples/ tree is the tracked
# master, mirrored here by `make samples` so the samples are runnable in the emulator
# and ship in the release zip. Ignore the mirror; edit samples/ instead.
drive/samples/

# BMX images staged into drive/ by hand to run a viewer or the paint benchmark.
# The masters are in samples/BMXVIEWER/SAMPLES; these are copies.
drive/*.BMX

# BASLOAD-GPC/build/ is cl65 output plus the emulator drive runtest.py makes: two bank images,
# a PRG, their maps, and a throwaway testdrive/. All of it comes back from
#     python BASLOAD-GPC/build.py both
# The VENDORED SOURCE under BASLOAD-GPC/upstream/ IS tracked -- it is a third-party BSD-2-Clause
# tree we build against, and the whole point of keeping it is that a fresh clone builds the same
# bytes without going back to GitHub.
BASLOAD-GPC/build/

# The packaged release zip -- built by release.sh / release.bat into the release/ drop
# folder as gpc-release-<n>.zip. Build artifact (matches in any folder), not committed.
gpc-release.zip
gpc-release-*.zip
drive/C.DIR.PRG
# Local compiler input config. Every automated consumer (bench/run-bench.sh,
# fixes/*/Makefile) writes its own, and `make release` overwrites it, so it is
# a working file, not shipped content. Untracked 2026-07-19.
drive/GPC.INPUT
/drive/XFMGR

# Compiled objects and the engine's compile log. GPC writes <name>.OBJ beside the source
# whenever anything here is compiled -- by the harnesses, by hand, or by the demos -- and
# every one of them is regenerable from the tracked .BASL/.PRG plus a build.
drive/*.OBJ
drive/GPCCOMP.LOG

# The two BUILD STAGES between a .BASL and a runnable object, for every program built
# in here: BASLOAD writes <name>.SRC.PRG and its <name>.SRC.SYM, GPC writes <name>.MAP.
# All three are regenerable from a tracked source plus a build.
#
# Globbed rather than named one at a time, because naming them one at a time is exactly
# how this went wrong: the bank tests BANKA..BANKZ.BASL ARE tracked, none of their four
# outputs was listed, and 114 untracked files had accumulated by 6th Sep 2026 -- one
# "git add -A" away from being committed, which is the mistake this whole section exists
# to prevent.
drive/*.SRC.PRG
drive/*.SRC.SYM
drive/*.MAP
drive/BANK*.PRG

# ...and the region overlays, <name>.nnn -- one file per GP.BANKED region, written beside
# the object by the same compile and named for its bank in three digits, .002 to .255. The
# same omission as above, one feature later: the overlays arrived after this section was
# written, so 120 of them had accumulated untracked by 8th Sep 2026. A region's count follows
# the source, so these can never be listed by name. Until 14th Sep 2026 the suffix was .Bnn.
drive/*.[0-9][0-9][0-9]

# Claude Code local settings (personal, not team config)
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/session-todo.json

# The runtime IMAGE, GPC.IMG.<build>.BIN -- what every self-contained object carries, streamed
# into it by WriteObjectCode. Generated by scripts/genrtimage.py from the first of the two links
# in source/application/Makefile, together with rtimage.gen.asm (the addresses and the GP-usage
# bitmap the compiler link needs).
#
# THE IMAGE ITSELF IS TRACKED, unlike the resident runtimes above, and for one reason:
# drive/GPC.BIN is tracked, and since the runtime moved out of it that compiler cannot
# produce a working program without this file beside it. The stale-binary argument that keeps
# GPB/GPC.RT.*.BIN out does not bite here -- the build number is pinned now (scripts/bumpbuild.py)
# and this is one file overwritten in place, not a new one per build. If rtbuild.txt is ever
# moved, git rm the orphan along with the rename.
source/application/rtimage.gen.asm
source/*/build/rtimage.prg
source/*/build/rtimage.lbl

# The per-feature emulator drives -- work/help, work/lineinput, work/rename, work/fileio.
# Each is a complete -fsroot: its own GPC.BIN, runtimes, library module copies and build.py, so a
# feature can be built and run WITHOUT contending for drive/, which is the one drive every other
# harness writes to. That is the whole reason they exist and why the module copies inside them are
# deliberate duplicates rather than drift to be reconciled.
#
# Regenerable, 1.2 MB, and full of binaries. NOT regenerable, and living only on this disk: the
# three build.py harnesses and the test sources TODO.md cites by name -- work/lineinput/EMUTST.BASL
# (the two-byte GETKEY probe), work/lineinput/KBTST.BASL (the undrained kbdbuf_put case),
# work/rename/LINTST.BASL and work/lineinput/LINTST2.BASL (the same six cases banked and unbanked).
/work/

# tmp-test/ is another throwaway emulator drive, made to build SVARS.BASL away from
# drive/: its own GPC.BIN, BASLOAD-GPC.BIN, runtimes and a handful of T*.BASL probes.
# Same class as /work above -- regenerable, all binaries, and one "git add -A" from being
# committed.
tmp-test/

# GPB.HELP is compiled SHARED and help-demo.bat mounts samples/GPC-HELP as its drive, so
# samplesbuild.py copies the current GPB/GPC.RT.nnn.BIN in beside the object -- the bootstrap
# looks for it at the drive root. Build output, build-numbered, cleared and re-copied on every
# build: the same reasoning that keeps the drive/ copies out. GPB.HELP.PRG itself IS tracked.
samples/GPC-HELP/GPB.RT.*.BIN
samples/GPC-HELP/GPC.RT.*.BIN
samples/GPC-HELP/GP1.RT.*.BIN
samples/GPC-HELP/GPC.RC.*.BIN

# release/ is TRACKED so the drop folder exists in a fresh clone, but its two products
# are not. release/TMP/ is the staging tree -- the release unpacked, wiped and rebuilt by
# "release.sh stage" -- and it also holds XFMGR and XT, which are the developer's own
# tools and never go in the zip. The zip itself matches the gpc-release-*.zip rule below.
release/TMP/

# OASIS/ is not part of this repository and never goes in git.
OASIS/
samples/GPC-HELP/GPB.HELP.SRC.SYM
samples/GPC-HELP/GPC.INPUT
samples/GPC-HELP/M.GPB.HELP.SRC.PRG
samples/GPC-HELP/GPC.INPUT
samples/GPC-HELP/D.GPB.HELP.SRC.PRG
samples/GPC-HELP/C.GPB.HELP.SRC.PRG
samples/GPC-HELP/GPB.HELP.SRC.PRG
samples/GPC-HELP/GPC.INPUT
/drive
samples/edit/EDIT.MAP
samples/edit/EDIT.SRC.SYM
samples/GPC-GUI-HELPER/GPC.GUI.MAP
samples/GPC-GUI-HELPER/GPC.GUI.SRC.SYM
samples/GPC-HELP/GPB.HELP.MAP
drive/EDITOR.SYM
drive/COLORTST.SYM
drive/BMXVIEW.SYM
drive/BMXVIEW.SYM
drive/COLORTST.SYM
drive/EDITOR.SYM

samples/*.zip

# The compile stages of a sample built in place: BASLOAD's symbol file, the GPC map and
# the M.<name> the compile leaves beside them. Regenerated by every samplesbuild.py run.
samples/*/*.MAP
samples/*/*.SRC.SYM
samples/*/M.*
